#### Problem 1: Session State Doesn't Scale in Distributed Systems

Traditional session-based authentication stores session data server-side and uses session IDs in cookies to retrieve user context. This creates fundamental scaling problems for Gainwell's distributed microservices:

```csharp
// Current problematic session-based approach
public async Task<IActionResult> GetClaim(string claimId)
{
    // Session lookup requires database call - 150ms overhead
    var session = await _sessionStore.GetSessionAsync(HttpContext.Session.Id);
    if (session == null || session.Expired)
        return Unauthorized();

    var userId = session.UserId;
    var roles = session.Roles; // Entire user context stored server-side

    // Every microservice call needs to pass session ID and look up context
    var claim = await _claimsService.GetClaimAsync(claimId, HttpContext.Session.Id);
    return Ok(claim);
}
```

**Consequences:**
- **Session database becomes bottleneck**: All APIs query session store, requiring expensive database capacity (current: 24-node Redis cluster handling 50K reads/sec)
- **Single point of failure**: Session store outage blocks all authentication (3 incidents in 2024, 14 hours total downtime)
- **Cross-region complexity**: Sessions must replicate across Azure regions (50-200ms replication lag causing intermittent auth failures)
- **Sticky sessions required**: Load balancers must pin users to specific servers, reducing scalability and complicating deployments
- **Microservices can't communicate**: Session IDs don't work for service-to-service calls requiring custom auth per service

