#### Problem 4: Token Signing and Validation Inconsistency

Different services use different signing algorithms, key management approaches, and validation logic:

```csharp
// Service A: HMAC-SHA256 symmetric signing
var key = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("MySecretKey123");  // Hardcoded!
var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
var tokenDescriptor = new SecurityTokenDescriptor
{
    Subject = new ClaimsIdentity(claims),
    SigningCredentials = new SigningCredentials(
        new SymmetricSecurityKey(key),
        SecurityAlgorithms.HmacSha256Signature
    )
};

// Service B: RSA-SHA256 asymmetric signing with different library
var rsa = RSA.Create();
rsa.ImportRSAPrivateKey(privateKeyBytes, out _);
var securityKey = new RsaSecurityKey(rsa);
// Different validation logic, different error handling

// Service C: No validation at all (trusts tokens blindly)
var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
var token = tokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(tokenString);  // No signature check!
var userId = token.Claims.First(c => c.Type == "sub").Value;
```

**Consequences:**
- **Hardcoded secrets**: HMAC keys hardcoded in configuration files (found in source control 3 times in 2024)
- **Key rotation impossible**: Changing signing keys requires coordinated deployment of 80+ microservices simultaneously
- **Algorithm confusion attacks**: Services accepting multiple algorithms vulnerable to algorithm substitution attacks
- **No signature validation**: Some services trust tokens without verifying signatures (14 services identified in security audit)
- **Performance variations**: Validation times range from 5ms to 250ms depending on implementation quality

